WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.357
The interquartile range, or IQR, is one simple statistical method for flagging unusually high or low values.

00:00:09.357 --> 00:00:17.367
First, we calculate Q1, the twenty-fifth percentile, and Q3, the seventy-fifth percentile.

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:21.552
The IQR is Q3 minus Q1.

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:27.660
Then we create lower and upper fences using one point five times the IQR.

00:00:28.396 --> 00:00:32.973
Run the code and inspect the values that fall outside those fences.

00:00:32.973 --> 00:00:35.853
The important word here is flagged.

00:00:35.853 --> 00:00:39.918
The IQR rule does not prove that a value is incorrect.

00:00:39.918 --> 00:00:45.418
It tells us that the value is unusual relative to the middle part of the distribution.

00:00:45.418 --> 00:00:50.668
This method is useful for screening, but context still matters.

00:00:50.668 --> 00:00:56.248
If the flagged value is eleven hundred, the next step might be checking the source record or understanding whether a

00:00:56.248 --> 00:01:00.439
different type of customer generated that order.
